1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
You thought you knew what the Fastlane was?

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Think again.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Imagine a superhighway designed for speed.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Thousands of miles of roadway unhindered by limits of any kind.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Where drivers can go as fast as their Porsches will carry them.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, the Autobahn on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Speed.

8
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:58,000
It's dangerous, exhilarating, intoxicating and illegal beyond clearly set limits that are posted on every major highway in the world.

9
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:05,000
except one

10
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:11,340
it's midnight at a gas station just off the German Autobahn in southern Bavaria

11
00:01:11,341 --> 00:01:15,220
we're about to live out every armchair speedsters fantasy

12
00:01:15,221 --> 00:01:19,220
to push the needle on the speedometer as far as it will go

13
00:01:19,221 --> 00:01:23,660
in this car the needle is going to go pretty far

14
00:01:23,661 --> 00:01:37,416
This car you see here was a car from Great Britain and the customer brings it to us as a stock 996 Turbo with 420 horsepower and we converted this car to 520

15
00:01:37,417 --> 00:01:38,380
horsepower.

16
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:48,660
Hans Lieb is a test driver for Roof Automotive, a boutique car company that converts high-end Porsches into the fastest street-legal cars in the world.

17
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:54,800
Everything about these cars, from the low friction tires to the super sensitive suspension

18
00:01:54,801 --> 00:02:00,280
to the massive engine, has been designed for Autobahn joyrides.

19
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:07,040
The roof car is definitely designed for the Autobahn because the roof car is so fast

20
00:02:07,041 --> 00:02:10,820
that it can only be driven such speeds on the Autobahn.

21
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:13,040
You cannot do that on a normal two-lane highway.

22
00:02:13,041 --> 00:02:22,300
Hans has to certify exactly how fast this $200,000 Porsche can go before sending it to its owner in London.

23
00:02:24,020 --> 00:02:31,420
The Autobahn is the ideal place and the only major highway in the world where he can legally make this kind of test run.

24
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:38,620
The Autobahn for us is a good road to test all our cars.

25
00:02:38,621 --> 00:02:43,220
We have mostly no speed limit and also it's in a really good condition.

26
00:02:43,221 --> 00:02:46,620
So it's really safe to test cars in this way by night.

27
00:02:46,621 --> 00:02:51,220
First you learn slowly to walk and then you learn faster to walk.

28
00:02:51,221 --> 00:02:54,220
And so it's the same impression you had with speed.

29
00:02:54,221 --> 00:02:58,720
Of course it's fun to go really, really fast with cars.

30
00:02:58,721 --> 00:03:03,720
But at the end you must be always really careful and your eyes must be watching all around.

31
00:03:03,721 --> 00:03:06,420
So also by side and also in front a little bit.

32
00:03:08,620 --> 00:03:17,920
At this speed, even the super smooth surface of the Autobahn, specially maintained to accommodate really fast drivers, feels rough and pitted.

33
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:26,480
And the slow and gentle Autobahn curves, designed to allow drivers to reach and hold extreme speeds, strain the Porsche.

34
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:37,500
The hardest part at that speed is if you see cars in front of you and you are not really sure how they react.

35
00:03:37,501 --> 00:03:41,500
Sometimes they are sleeping and they are turning to the middle line.

36
00:03:41,501 --> 00:03:48,500
So you must be really sure that the line is clear and that it is not a car who can make something wrong in front of you.

37
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:04,500
You must be really concentrated at that speed because the car runs over 200 or 300 meters per second.

38
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:29,300
This 520 horsepower Porsche tops out at 7200 RPMs and 212 miles per hour.

39
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:34,240
That's with a camera crew of two squeezed into the side and rear bucket seats.

40
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:47,400
Welcome to the Autobahn, the only freeway in the world where 212, a speed-to-police helicopters

41
00:04:47,401 --> 00:04:52,620
can't match, is perfectly legal.

42
00:04:52,621 --> 00:04:57,620
This is the ultimate driver's fantasy land, not just for professionals, but for anyone

43
00:04:57,621 --> 00:04:58,620
with a license.

44
00:04:58,621 --> 00:05:04,460
A kind of bizarro world where all the basics are in reverse.

45
00:05:04,461 --> 00:05:06,460
It's actually illegal to drive too slow.

46
00:05:06,461 --> 00:05:14,460
But there are no limits at all for thousands of miles of Autobahn on how fast you can go.

47
00:05:14,461 --> 00:05:21,500
As hard as it is to get used to, it's actually okay to pass a cop here at speeds that would

48
00:05:21,501 --> 00:05:26,260
land you in prison in the U.S.

49
00:05:26,261 --> 00:05:30,580
There is that initial hesitation the first time you see the Autobahn police.

50
00:05:30,581 --> 00:05:35,640
I remember talking to my friends and saying, you know, well, we can't get a ticket, can we?

51
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:40,840
And then just simply passing and, you know, them at, you know, like 110 miles an hour,

52
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:45,060
they never gave us a second look because we weren't doing anything illegal.

53
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:53,600
The Autobahn turns the basic premise that speed kills on its head.

54
00:05:54,520 --> 00:05:56,600
Weird and counterintuitive as it seems.

55
00:05:57,440 --> 00:05:59,880
Driving without limits, at least as it's practiced in Germany,

56
00:06:00,580 --> 00:06:06,240
is as safe and possibly safer than the more conservative approach favored by the rest of the world.

57
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:15,460
I think generally the average American looks at the Autobahn as, you know, Germany's way of population control.

58
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:23,300
And ultimately, if you look at it statistically, it's very safe to drive on the Autobahn,

59
00:06:23,301 --> 00:06:29,440
and especially the last 10, 15 years, it's scored consistently a lower death rate than our American interstate.

60
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:32,087
But how is this possible?

61
00:06:32,088 --> 00:06:39,020
One can't help but think on the face of it that a freeway on which people can go 212 would be incredibly dangerous.

62
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:40,460
Not so.

63
00:06:40,461 --> 00:06:46,920
In exchange for the right to fly, Autobahn drivers have to sacrifice other freedoms.

64
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:56,760
The Germans permit much higher speeds but force much more rigid controls in how you behave on the highways in terms of lane discipline.

65
00:06:56,761 --> 00:07:04,480
Here, you can meander all over all the lanes wherever you want to go, provided you drive within the posted limits.

66
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:09,400
They have a law that they call, it's Rechtsfahren, drive right.

67
00:07:09,540 --> 00:07:14,300
You can only drive in the right-hand lane and pass in the left, and that's the law.

68
00:07:14,380 --> 00:07:16,920
If you pass in the right, you can be pulled over and given a ticket.

69
00:07:17,460 --> 00:07:19,640
And so everybody rigidly observes this.

70
00:07:19,641 --> 00:07:26,800
So the slower traffic is always to the right, and there's never a left lane bandit or a slowpoke sitting in the left lane blocking traffic.

71
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:30,420
Autobahn drivers don't just drive right.

72
00:07:31,320 --> 00:07:37,860
They drive with a distinctive concentration and focus, one that 100-plus miles an hour has a way of quickly inducing.

73
00:07:38,760 --> 00:07:44,620
There is no multitasking on the Autobahn, no talking on the cell phone, no eating on the run.

74
00:07:44,621 --> 00:07:48,760
German companies never understood why we needed cup holders,

75
00:07:48,761 --> 00:07:51,540
because driving is an activity in itself.

76
00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:55,080
One takes pride and has to put full concentration in driving.

77
00:07:55,120 --> 00:08:00,560
It's not something you do while slurping on a 7-Up, as we Americans.

78
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:04,840
That whole idea of the cup holder, in a way,

79
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:09,680
illustrates the difference between the way the automobile in the United States

80
00:08:09,681 --> 00:08:13,220
is wrapped into the very fabric of everyday life, eating and drinking,

81
00:08:13,221 --> 00:08:19,680
whereas it's an important part of German life, but it is pursued almost for its own sake.

82
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:25,320
Another factor that makes the Autobahn safe at any speed is the road itself.

83
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,480
The Autobahn is more than twice as thick as the American interstate,

84
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:33,260
27 inches of asphalt and concrete, which prevents cracking.

85
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:36,200
And maintenance is extraordinary.

86
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:40,300
When a crack does appear, the entire section of road is quickly replaced,

87
00:08:40,301 --> 00:08:44,040
which is why maintenance slowdowns are a frequent occurrence.

88
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:48,100
As a rule, the Autobahn is incredibly smooth.

89
00:08:50,160 --> 00:08:55,160
You can't have potholes on a road where you can drive 120 or faster.

90
00:08:56,640 --> 00:08:58,860
Structural features also make a difference,

91
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:03,120
especially the double-sided crash guardrails

92
00:09:03,121 --> 00:09:06,880
that separate opposing lines of traffic and prevent head-on collisions.

93
00:09:06,881 --> 00:09:10,180
Most of the American interstate has a weaker single barrier.

94
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:13,300
or none at all.

95
00:09:13,301 --> 00:09:18,140
And it's much harder to get a driver's license here. Individual driver's training

96
00:09:18,141 --> 00:09:19,060
is mandatory.

97
00:09:19,061 --> 00:09:22,300
And the license itself, which you have to be

98
00:09:22,301 --> 00:09:25,700
18 to get, costs between fifteen hundred

99
00:09:25,701 --> 00:09:31,480
and two thousand dollars. A key feature of the Autobahn safety net, of course,

100
00:09:31,481 --> 00:09:36,180
is the quality of German cars. Cars designed from the 1930s on,

101
00:09:36,181 --> 00:09:40,180
with the Autobahn in mind.

102
00:09:40,181 --> 00:09:46,800
Cars that can go incredibly fast, but that are also beautifully maneuverable at extreme speeds.

103
00:09:48,340 --> 00:09:55,140
And as this footage shot by the German Highway Patrol illustrates, cars that can save a life at any speed.

104
00:09:58,100 --> 00:10:09,280
I would credit the unlimited speed limits of the German Autobahns for not only creating very highly engineered and effective automobiles, but very safe automobiles.

105
00:10:09,281 --> 00:10:19,894
The suspensions, the gearboxes, the brakes, the steering, the lights, the structure of these cars became the best in the world in many ways because they were tested in

106
00:10:19,895 --> 00:10:23,580
the crucible of high-speed running on the German Autobahn.

107
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:30,940
That environment is increasingly threatened.

108
00:10:31,960 --> 00:10:34,940
At one time, there wasn't a single speed sign on the Autobahn.

109
00:10:35,760 --> 00:10:39,680
Now with urban sprawl and 50 million German trucks and cars,

110
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:42,840
nearly half of the Autobahn is heavily signed.

111
00:10:45,820 --> 00:10:47,720
But outside the major cities,

112
00:10:48,420 --> 00:10:52,080
the Autobahn still affords plenty of opportunities for ordinary drivers

113
00:10:52,081 --> 00:10:54,600
to open up an experience of freedom of the road

114
00:10:54,601 --> 00:10:57,800
that the rest of the world can only fantasize about.

115
00:10:57,801 --> 00:11:09,240
100 110 miles an hour is perfectly easy to do and very very comfortable and i find it

116
00:11:09,241 --> 00:11:14,860
about the the normal speed although if you're driving 100 miles an hour in germany on the open

117
00:11:14,861 --> 00:11:19,860
autobahn you better watch your rearview mirror and stay right because you may find a bmw or mercedes

118
00:11:19,861 --> 00:11:26,740
or a porsche go by in as much as 130 140 miles an hour the first time you get on the autobahn is

119
00:11:26,741 --> 00:11:30,420
It's just an exhilarating experience.

120
00:11:30,421 --> 00:11:35,160
You see that sign, the round sign with the diagonal lines through it, which is the end

121
00:11:35,161 --> 00:11:36,160
of speed restrictions.

122
00:11:36,161 --> 00:11:50,161
You are now on a road that has no speed limit.

123
00:11:52,780 --> 00:11:56,320
Listening to Beethoven as you drive 110 miles an hour down the Autobahn

124
00:11:56,321 --> 00:12:01,760
is just one of the life-affirming experiences that you can have if you're a car buff.

125
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:05,000
You're supposed to test your car, test yourself, drive faster.

126
00:12:05,240 --> 00:12:06,980
Get places, of course, fast.

127
00:12:07,860 --> 00:12:09,340
The road is somewhat of an adventure,

128
00:12:10,340 --> 00:12:12,780
and I think it was designed to be that from the very beginning.

129
00:12:13,520 --> 00:12:17,660
An adventure that had its roots in the deadliest hour of German history.

130
00:12:17,661 --> 00:12:23,900
Up next, the origins of what was once officially known as Adolf Hitler's Road.

131
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:31,840
There are .72 fatalities per 100 million vehicle miles traveled on the Autobahn,

132
00:12:31,841 --> 00:12:34,940
compared to .84 on the U.S. Interstate.

133
00:12:34,941 --> 00:12:37,980
The Autobahn will return on Modern Marvels.

134
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:44,480
We now return to the Autobahn on Modern Marvels.

135
00:12:44,481 --> 00:12:49,760
The Autobahn is one of the most beautifully groomed highways in the world.

136
00:12:50,300 --> 00:12:51,800
It looks like it was built yesterday.

137
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:55,520
A state-of-the-art road for state-of-the-art cars.

138
00:12:56,320 --> 00:13:00,480
But the Autobahn is actually the oldest national freeway system in the world.

139
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:05,720
Its roots go back to Adolf Hitler and the Third Reich.

140
00:13:06,240 --> 00:13:12,660
In fact, until the end of World War II, the Autobahn's official name was Die Straßen Adolf Hitlers,

141
00:13:12,661 --> 00:13:14,660
or Adolf Hitler's Road.

142
00:13:16,060 --> 00:13:18,820
Built between 1933 and 1942,

143
00:13:19,700 --> 00:13:22,700
the Autobahn was the largest and most triumphantly publicized

144
00:13:22,701 --> 00:13:24,900
public works project of the Nazi regime.

145
00:13:25,420 --> 00:13:27,560
It put hundreds of thousands of people to work,

146
00:13:28,040 --> 00:13:30,100
made blitzkrieg military movements possible,

147
00:13:30,700 --> 00:13:33,080
and served as a symbol of the new Germany,

148
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:35,280
the Germany that could get things done.

149
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:40,300
A high Nazi official has quoted saying that everything in Germany in the 30s

150
00:13:40,301 --> 00:13:43,460
had to move at 100 miles per hour, and therefore you needed the autobots.

151
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:47,360
Germany could project itself to its people and to the rest of the world

152
00:13:47,361 --> 00:13:51,620
as a country on the move, moving into the future faster than anyone else.

153
00:13:51,940 --> 00:13:53,740
This was reflected in the streamlined cars.

154
00:13:53,860 --> 00:13:56,620
It was reflected in the curves of the autobots.

155
00:13:58,600 --> 00:14:03,340
The myth about Adolf Hitler's road was that Hitler himself came up with the idea

156
00:14:03,341 --> 00:14:05,700
for a high-speed, cars-only freeway,

157
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:09,840
while serving a five-year jail term for the failed Beer Hall Putsch

158
00:14:09,841 --> 00:14:11,560
in Munich in 1923.

159
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,280
In fact, German engineers had developed the basic paradigm

160
00:14:16,281 --> 00:14:18,720
for the modern freeway when they began designing

161
00:14:18,721 --> 00:14:21,980
experimental limited access roads for Berlin and other cities

162
00:14:21,981 --> 00:14:23,640
in 1913.

163
00:14:24,360 --> 00:14:27,240
The elements of that are so familiar to us today,

164
00:14:27,420 --> 00:14:30,840
it's worthy to repeat that they were inventions at one time.

165
00:14:31,100 --> 00:14:35,180
That is, dividing traffic with a median in both directions,

166
00:14:35,181 --> 00:14:38,340
having at least two lanes so that the faster traffic

167
00:14:38,341 --> 00:14:45,620
can pass the slower traffic and having pavement that is solid and curves that are banked and

168
00:14:45,621 --> 00:14:48,700
designed in a sufficient radius for the handling of the vehicle.

169
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:56,540
At first, the National Socialist Party attacked the Autobahn as an elitist extravagance that

170
00:14:56,541 --> 00:14:59,720
would only benefit the tiny percentage of Germans who owned cars.

171
00:15:02,060 --> 00:15:08,060
But that changed when Hitler came to power and grasped its political, social, propagandistic

172
00:15:08,061 --> 00:15:09,520
and imperial value.

173
00:15:10,740 --> 00:15:14,560
It was understood that they would extend to what Hitler called Greater Germany,

174
00:15:14,680 --> 00:15:17,380
and that meant the parts of Europe he was planning to take over.

175
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:20,680
So the one that ran to the east would eventually go all the way to Moscow.

176
00:15:21,620 --> 00:15:26,540
And the Autobahns, as the roads had been for the Roman Empire,

177
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:31,200
were to be the skeleton for this new great German Empire, the Third Reich.

178
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:37,280
Groundbreaking on the Reich's Autobahn took place on September 23, 1933.

179
00:15:38,060 --> 00:15:44,780
just south of frankfurt hitler himself wielded the shovel on the planned 6900 kilometer or 4300

180
00:15:44,781 --> 00:15:50,860
mile network of roads within months 130 000 unemployed germans many of them white collar

181
00:15:50,861 --> 00:15:56,220
workers from cities were handed shovels and sent to the countryside to live in work camps and clear

182
00:15:56,221 --> 00:16:02,540
land virtually all of the initial work was done by hand partly because the germans didn't possess

183
00:16:02,541 --> 00:16:08,920
road-clearing technology, partly because Nazi ideology glorified the virtues of manual labor.

184
00:16:10,300 --> 00:16:16,480
The people who actually did the construction, very frequently idealized in the propaganda

185
00:16:16,481 --> 00:16:23,100
and the wonderful pictures of these bronzed workers heroically struggling, it was actually

186
00:16:23,101 --> 00:16:24,460
a failing, miserable job.

187
00:16:24,461 --> 00:16:28,160
When the weather was good, they would work them not just eight hours

188
00:16:28,161 --> 00:16:29,568
a day, but 10 or 12 hours a day.

189
00:16:29,569 --> 00:16:32,340
A lot of people simply weren't in shape for this sort of thing.

190
00:16:32,341 --> 00:16:35,680
And they weren't construction guys, they were office people and factory people,

191
00:16:35,820 --> 00:16:37,640
and then all of a sudden now they're out in the elements.

192
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:41,380
They had strikes in 1934 in some of the camps,

193
00:16:41,640 --> 00:16:43,840
and the Nazis came down very hard on them.

194
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:45,740
They sent, I mean, they sent the Gestapo.

195
00:16:46,400 --> 00:16:49,220
And they wanted to know who was a former communist, who was a troublemaker.

196
00:16:50,280 --> 00:16:54,460
Some workers were so shocked by conditions, they sent naive letters to Hitler.

197
00:16:55,560 --> 00:16:58,120
Mein Führer, if only you knew, they wrote.

198
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,220
The letters were turned over to the Gestapo.

199
00:17:02,340 --> 00:17:04,240
which opened files on the men who wrote them.

200
00:17:06,840 --> 00:17:10,240
Land clearing was particularly difficult because Fritz Thoth,

201
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:12,580
the man Hitler put in charge of construction,

202
00:17:13,160 --> 00:17:16,280
wanted the Autobahn to be both utilitarian and beautiful,

203
00:17:16,960 --> 00:17:19,880
a showcase for the glories of German landscape and culture.

204
00:17:21,580 --> 00:17:25,460
The evils of National Socialism, curiously, were not at odds

205
00:17:25,461 --> 00:17:28,040
with a passionate love of a scenic drive.

206
00:17:28,041 --> 00:17:34,160
Germans have always been tremendously romantic about nature in every way and they very much

207
00:17:34,161 --> 00:17:39,776
envision that the Audubons would be roads that would be at home in the landscape.

208
00:17:39,777 --> 00:17:40,477
There are

209
00:17:40,478 --> 00:17:48,980
many propaganda posters that show the sort of lovely vistas from the curves or high points

210
00:17:48,981 --> 00:17:56,540
on the Audubon and all this at a time that slave laborers were being used in the war factories and

211
00:17:56,541 --> 00:17:58,580
and people were being rounded up into camps.

212
00:17:58,960 --> 00:18:04,040
So it was a very bizarre and paranoid and ultimately schizophrenic vision.

213
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,080
Bridges were a big part of the Autobahn vision.

214
00:18:07,640 --> 00:18:12,600
More than a thousand bridges, including monumental suspension and arch spans.

215
00:18:13,660 --> 00:18:17,480
Nearly a third of the cost of the Autobahn went into bridge construction.

216
00:18:18,660 --> 00:18:23,060
Some of the earliest ones were built of steel,

217
00:18:23,061 --> 00:18:29,040
but after 1936 with rearmament steel was in short supply so they wanted to use more stonework

218
00:18:29,041 --> 00:18:35,560
and they designed and built some very spectacular i mean the greatest stone bridges since the romans

219
00:18:35,561 --> 00:18:41,580
great viaducts across valleys all all stonework which no one's ever done since

220
00:18:41,581 --> 00:18:48,640
the first stretch of autobahn a 37 mile strip from frankfurt to darmstadt

221
00:18:48,641 --> 00:18:52,480
was open to traffic on May 19, 1935.

222
00:18:53,160 --> 00:18:54,860
Hitler presided over the ribbon cutting.

223
00:18:57,800 --> 00:19:00,840
It would serve as a prototype for the rest of the system,

224
00:19:01,480 --> 00:19:05,160
an elegant double-lane highway divided by a narrow median strip.

225
00:19:05,640 --> 00:19:07,500
The highway itself was made of concrete,

226
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:09,640
with cobblestone entry ramps

227
00:19:09,641 --> 00:19:12,300
and overpasses at regular intervals.

228
00:19:14,180 --> 00:19:16,340
The overpass was a key innovation

229
00:19:16,341 --> 00:19:20,740
that made the Autobahn and the modern speedway really fast.

230
00:19:21,440 --> 00:19:26,200
The great enemy of speed in an automobile is the stop sign and the stoplight.

231
00:19:26,600 --> 00:19:29,600
The idea of the Autobahn was that you never had to stop.

232
00:19:29,820 --> 00:19:35,200
Therefore, we have the cloverleaf board, the overpass, the on-ramp and the off-ramps,

233
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:39,420
all those things that we take so much for granted as part of normal divided highways.

234
00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:41,380
But that was a very new idea.

235
00:19:42,160 --> 00:19:45,020
From the start, the new roads were designed for speed.

236
00:19:45,021 --> 00:19:50,520
original specs assumed an upper limit of 100 miles per hour which made posted

237
00:19:50,521 --> 00:19:54,900
speed limits unnecessary since virtually no cars could drive that fast

238
00:19:54,901 --> 00:19:59,540
grades were limited to seven percent which meant they could rise or fall no

239
00:19:59,541 --> 00:20:05,480
more than seven feet in any 100 foot stretch and curves were extremely gentle

240
00:20:05,481 --> 00:20:10,400
and long so the drivers barely had to touch the steering wheel and never had

241
00:20:10,401 --> 00:20:11,299
to slow down.

242
00:20:11,300 --> 00:20:16,900
The Germans were eager to showcase their innovative new speedway, and the Audubon

243
00:20:16,901 --> 00:20:18,080
made a striking impression.

244
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:24,700
Foreign visitors who were brought in and given tours, and many of them, and carried over

245
00:20:24,701 --> 00:20:30,300
the Audubon system in zeppelins or driven along in high-speed vehicles, were amazed.

246
00:20:30,480 --> 00:20:34,360
And they would comment, for instance, that they were able to sit and take notes at 80

247
00:20:34,361 --> 00:20:35,624
or 90 miles an hour.

248
00:20:35,625 --> 00:20:39,860
And this was the first that most people in other parts of the world

249
00:20:39,861 --> 00:20:43,960
had heard of the idea of a modern high-speed automobile highway.

250
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:50,900
Ironically, the most modern, sleek, ambitious, beautiful, and expensive freeway system in the world

251
00:20:50,901 --> 00:20:54,480
was virtually empty most of the time.

252
00:20:55,500 --> 00:20:59,080
Just one in 75 Germans owned a car in 1936,

253
00:20:59,081 --> 00:21:04,300
and the Autobahn carried, on average, less than a single car per minute per kilometer.

254
00:21:04,301 --> 00:21:13,660
Hitler sought to remedy the problem by commissioning Ferdinand Porsche to design a car that ordinary Germans could drive on the Autobahn.

255
00:21:14,920 --> 00:21:20,620
The Volkswagen, which would sell for just $360, went into production in 1938.

256
00:21:21,260 --> 00:21:25,540
But only 800 vehicles were finished when war broke out and the factory was closed.

257
00:21:26,900 --> 00:21:29,520
The Volkswagen wasn't the only car designed for the Autobahn.

258
00:21:29,521 --> 00:21:37,520
Other models were built for luxury and speed, like this Autobahn wagon built by Adler Automotive in 1936.

259
00:21:37,521 --> 00:21:42,520
Actual racing cars could also sometimes be seen flying along the speedway.

260
00:21:42,521 --> 00:21:52,520
In 1938, Rudy Caracciola set a world speed mark of 268.9 miles per hour on a stretch of Autobahn outside Stuttgart.

261
00:21:52,521 --> 00:21:57,520
Normally, record speedruns are made on broad expanses of flat land,

262
00:21:57,521 --> 00:22:02,520
but Hitler insisted on using the 24.6 foot wide Autobahn.

263
00:22:02,521 --> 00:22:07,520
Caracciola, who survived it, later said that it was like aiming down the barrel of a gun.

264
00:22:07,521 --> 00:22:13,520
At 270 miles an hour, the overpasses would come up every few seconds,

265
00:22:13,521 --> 00:22:22,520
so that the skill levels to drive a car that fast in 1937 was miraculous.

266
00:22:22,521 --> 00:22:31,520
Later that day, Bernd Rosemeyer, Germany's most celebrated racer, attempted to top Caracciola

267
00:22:31,521 --> 00:22:33,520
with disastrous results.

268
00:22:33,521 --> 00:22:40,820
He was hit by a crosswind at an estimated 270 miles an hour, and the car was blown off

269
00:22:40,821 --> 00:22:46,520
the Autobahn, tumbled four or five hundred yards, shredded itself into a thousand pieces,

270
00:22:46,521 --> 00:22:49,920
and Rosemeyer was killed.

271
00:22:49,921 --> 00:22:52,880
ended any record attempts on the Autobahn.

272
00:22:54,400 --> 00:22:58,440
The race to complete the Autobahn, however, continued at a grueling pace.

273
00:23:00,040 --> 00:23:05,140
By May 1939, 3,000 kilometers of Autobahn were ready for traffic.

274
00:23:06,480 --> 00:23:12,260
Ideology had now given way to technology, and most Autobahn work was done with machines.

275
00:23:13,160 --> 00:23:19,200
They could design the machinery because all the roads were exactly the same size, so they

276
00:23:19,201 --> 00:23:24,960
They built a very efficient construction train of various machines all going along,

277
00:23:25,480 --> 00:23:30,320
clearing the land and putting stuff down and laying the different stages of the pavement.

278
00:23:30,560 --> 00:23:33,200
And it was all very efficiently done.

279
00:23:34,680 --> 00:23:38,540
Construction tailed off abruptly as Germany geared up for war.

280
00:23:38,541 --> 00:23:44,480
And only a few hundred miles were added by 1942 when work on the Autobahn came to a standstill.

281
00:23:44,481 --> 00:23:52,060
As a military speedway, the Autobahn proved to be of little value, although propaganda pictures suggested otherwise.

282
00:23:53,380 --> 00:24:00,160
The rails were actually more efficient for moving equipment and troops, since they extended farther beyond German borders.

283
00:24:01,580 --> 00:24:06,500
As the war turned desperate, however, the Autobahn did play an unexpected role.

284
00:24:07,420 --> 00:24:10,040
Oddly enough, they were used as airports.

285
00:24:10,041 --> 00:24:19,040
The Germans hid fighter planes in the woods nearby, came out, closed the highway and used them as landing and take-off strips.

286
00:24:19,041 --> 00:24:25,040
And there were about 30 sites all around Germany that there were different emergency airports.

287
00:24:25,041 --> 00:24:35,040
Ironically, the Autobahn proved to be very useful to American GIs who rolled through Germany in 1944 on the world's best roads.

288
00:24:35,041 --> 00:24:44,760
By the end of the war, the Autobahn was heavily damaged, not by the Allies, but by the retreating

289
00:24:44,761 --> 00:24:50,040
Germans who blew up virtually all the bridges that had so recently and painstakingly been

290
00:24:50,041 --> 00:24:56,680
built. Remarkably, the bridges would soon be built up again, along with the rest of

291
00:24:56,681 --> 00:25:04,566
the Autobahn. Next, the famous freeway paves the way to post-war reconstruction.

292
00:25:04,567 --> 00:25:05,267
With

293
00:25:05,268 --> 00:25:13,320
With labor in short supply during the war, the Germans used prisoners of war and forced labor to construct Autobahn extensions in Poland and Eastern Europe.

294
00:25:14,760 --> 00:25:17,900
The Autobahn will return on Modern Marvels.

295
00:25:19,160 --> 00:25:25,400
In the aftermath of defeat in World War II, the Autobahn finally proved useful to ordinary Germans.

296
00:25:26,980 --> 00:25:31,500
Most of the vehicles on the Autobahn were military, American military.

297
00:25:31,501 --> 00:25:37,500
German drivers didn't reappear until the early 1950s.

298
00:25:37,501 --> 00:25:41,500
By then, the country had splintered into East and West Germany.

299
00:25:41,501 --> 00:25:48,500
In the West, the Volkswagen, which had been shelved during the war, reemerged as the symbol of post-war recovery.

300
00:25:48,501 --> 00:25:52,500
Both the car and the Autobahn were taking on new meaning.

301
00:25:52,501 --> 00:25:57,500
The Autobahn's have a post-war symbolism too.

302
00:25:57,501 --> 00:26:00,900
It's not so much an official orchestrated thing as it was in the 30s.

303
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:05,120
It becomes somewhat of a consumer symbol that everybody had their Volkswagen and they want to go someplace.

304
00:26:05,440 --> 00:26:09,460
It was like, you know, we want cars, we want roads, we want to be like the Americans.

305
00:26:10,360 --> 00:26:15,540
With one exception, the Germans still wanted to drive on the Autobahn as fast as possible.

306
00:26:16,840 --> 00:26:24,160
Posted speed limits were non-existent and gradients were lowered from a pre-war maximum of 7% to just 4%

307
00:26:24,161 --> 00:26:26,560
so the drivers wouldn't slow down going uphill.

308
00:26:27,500 --> 00:26:34,140
The speed challenge of the Autobahn, meanwhile, was a big influence on car design, along with the price of gas.

309
00:26:34,760 --> 00:26:43,560
Fuel economy would also be a consideration for the German auto industry to build cars that are both fuel efficient and high performance.

310
00:26:44,040 --> 00:26:51,060
That's why you wound up with these little Porsches with these tiny little motors that would scream down the Autobahn at 100 miles per hour.

311
00:26:51,061 --> 00:26:57,940
Plus, the need for high speed stability, very tight suspensions and steering, good tires and brakes.

312
00:26:58,380 --> 00:27:04,260
All of these technological advancements showed up in mass in Europe first.

313
00:27:05,020 --> 00:27:10,260
By the mid-1950s, most of the pre-war Autobahn in West Germany had been restored.

314
00:27:10,860 --> 00:27:17,080
And the West German government began the first of three four-year plans to build major extensions and new roads.

315
00:27:17,081 --> 00:27:24,400
It came out with what you would call the modern version of the German Autobahn, where you

316
00:27:24,401 --> 00:27:30,580
had a hard shoulder, the driving lanes, the center median with a continuous double guard

317
00:27:30,581 --> 00:27:33,260
rail, the early Autobahn with no guard rail.

318
00:27:33,261 --> 00:27:36,700
You could jump over that and have a gruesome crash.

319
00:27:36,701 --> 00:27:44,980
Intriguingly, as Autobahn expansion was underway, America began building the 44,000 mile interstate,

320
00:27:44,981 --> 00:27:48,220
the largest road system of all time.

321
00:27:48,221 --> 00:27:52,540
President Eisenhower, who had seen the Autobahn firsthand as commander of allied forces in

322
00:27:52,541 --> 00:28:00,980
Europe, was the prime mover behind the interstate, which modeled itself heavily on the Autobahn.

323
00:28:00,981 --> 00:28:06,220
Like the Autobahn, Eisenhower sold the interstate as a national defense highway that could move

324
00:28:06,221 --> 00:28:10,640
American weapons and troops, and that could serve as an escape route in case of a nuclear

325
00:28:10,641 --> 00:28:13,500
attack.

326
00:28:13,501 --> 00:28:17,240
One major difference, of course, was in the area of speed.

327
00:28:17,241 --> 00:28:21,980
With the exception of a handful of western states, the interstate was built with limits,

328
00:28:21,981 --> 00:28:25,860
usually between 65 and 75 miles an hour.

329
00:28:25,861 --> 00:28:31,060
One reason that our highways came to have speed limits was that they were regulated by

330
00:28:31,061 --> 00:28:32,300
local authorities.

331
00:28:32,301 --> 00:28:36,060
We did not have a federal system as the autobots were.

332
00:28:36,061 --> 00:28:40,860
But at the same time also, there were differences in automobile technology.

333
00:28:40,861 --> 00:28:44,860
While Cadillac at that point was calling itself the standard of the world, it did not have

334
00:28:44,861 --> 00:28:51,520
a lot of the technical innovations that Mercedes, BMW, and even less expensive German cars had

335
00:28:51,521 --> 00:28:53,380
incorporated decades before.

336
00:28:54,360 --> 00:28:58,100
The interstate wasn't just slower than the Autobahn, it was cheaper.

337
00:29:00,820 --> 00:29:06,880
With over 40,000 miles of roadway to construct, engineers opted for a thinner 11-inch road

338
00:29:06,881 --> 00:29:10,414
bed that would begin to break down after 20 years.

339
00:29:10,415 --> 00:29:14,160
The 27-inch Autobahn was built to last twice as long.

340
00:29:16,560 --> 00:29:22,240
American engineers also favored what seemed like a no-frills, utilitarian design for the interstate,

341
00:29:22,241 --> 00:29:24,080
with as many straight lines as possible.

342
00:29:26,400 --> 00:29:32,000
The lesson of that highway was brought home almost immediately as drivers began to suffer

343
00:29:32,001 --> 00:29:34,140
from what was called highway hypnosis.

344
00:29:34,860 --> 00:29:37,340
Straight highways, it turned out, would put drivers to sleep.

345
00:29:37,460 --> 00:29:41,960
So it turned out that the curves, the things that seemed simply aesthetics

346
00:29:41,961 --> 00:29:46,580
when they were designed in the Autobahns in Germany to fit the landscape

347
00:29:46,581 --> 00:29:50,500
were also very useful for safety.

348
00:29:52,300 --> 00:29:55,800
Safety became a major concern as the volume of traffic rose.

349
00:29:56,440 --> 00:29:59,860
By 1970, there were 14 million cars in West Germany

350
00:29:59,861 --> 00:30:06,400
and 19,000 fatalities a year, many of the most gruesome, of course, on Germany's fastest road.

351
00:30:09,620 --> 00:30:15,520
Authorities raised fines and added jail time for drunk driving, hit and run, and other traffic violations,

352
00:30:16,240 --> 00:30:25,000
made seatbelt use mandatory, and eventually posted a suggested nationwide speed limit of 130 kilometers, or 81 miles an hour.

353
00:30:26,800 --> 00:30:29,200
Meanwhile, the Autobahn continued to grow.

354
00:30:29,860 --> 00:30:37,060
The 1970s saw a level of expansion equal to that of the 30s, with more than 3,000 kilometers added to the highway web.

355
00:30:39,340 --> 00:30:44,260
Planners wanted to put an Autobahn entry road within 10 kilometers of any point in Germany.

356
00:30:45,620 --> 00:30:53,080
By 1990, the Autobahn had grown to more than 11,000 kilometers, four times the amount that had been built before the war.

357
00:30:53,081 --> 00:31:03,960
A growing number of Germans began to see the Autobahn not as a national icon, but as a sprawling monster spewing pollution and choking the land.

358
00:31:05,820 --> 00:31:15,680
Next, managing that monster, balancing the high-speed expectations of millions of drivers against the creeping reality of the Autobahn at rush hour.

359
00:31:15,681 --> 00:31:25,300
A national speed limit of 100 kilometers per hour, about 63 miles per hour, was imposed

360
00:31:25,301 --> 00:31:30,120
on the Autobahn on November 24, 1973 during the oil crisis.

361
00:31:30,121 --> 00:31:32,440
It lasted less than four months.

362
00:31:32,441 --> 00:31:37,720
The Autobahn will return on Modern Marvels.

363
00:31:37,721 --> 00:31:42,940
Although speed has made the Autobahn famous, the reality of Autobahn driving can be anything

364
00:31:42,941 --> 00:31:45,220
but fast.

365
00:31:45,221 --> 00:31:49,560
Many sections of the Autobahn are like rush hour in Chicago or LA.

366
00:31:49,561 --> 00:31:54,720
And since the early 1990s, German authorities have employed a sophisticated system of high-tech

367
00:31:54,721 --> 00:31:59,540
surveillance and feedback to keep things flowing.

368
00:31:59,541 --> 00:32:05,380
And they have imposed speed limits, variable speed limits, the change according to the

369
00:32:05,381 --> 00:32:07,420
conditions of the road.

370
00:32:07,421 --> 00:32:12,100
The idea is to overcome this bottleneck with a system like this that limits the speed for

371
00:32:12,101 --> 00:32:18,820
certain time during the urban areas to a synchronized traffic of 100 kilometers per hour, 120 possible.

372
00:32:19,940 --> 00:32:24,980
The speed limits and other driving directives are triggered by sensors in and on the Autobahn

373
00:32:26,500 --> 00:32:31,140
that shoot weather and traffic data to roadside computers every 60 seconds.

374
00:32:32,100 --> 00:32:36,740
When, for example, the sensors determine that over 15 percent of Autobahn traffic is trucks

375
00:32:36,741 --> 00:32:39,920
and that speeds have slowed to 80 kilometers an hour,

376
00:32:40,540 --> 00:32:44,360
a no-passing symbol for trucks is flashed on the overhead signs.

377
00:32:45,580 --> 00:32:47,400
Speed limits are introduced gradually,

378
00:32:47,840 --> 00:32:50,340
five miles in advance of an actual slowdown,

379
00:32:50,600 --> 00:32:53,600
so the cars don't fly headlong into a massive jam.

380
00:32:54,620 --> 00:32:56,320
It's an effective system.

381
00:32:56,321 --> 00:33:02,560
We had a reduction of accidents in the first three years of roughly 30%.

382
00:33:02,561 --> 00:33:11,940
There's no better view of just how slow the world's fastest freeway system can get than here, the traffic control center for southern Bavaria.

383
00:33:13,980 --> 00:33:20,700
Over 220 cameras monitor traffic on the Autobahn around Munich, especially in tunnels and over bridges.

384
00:33:23,220 --> 00:33:32,360
On the screen and on the video, we have mostly very dense traffic, so the high-speed drivers have no chance.

385
00:33:32,560 --> 00:33:36,960
to drive as high as they like to do.

386
00:33:37,640 --> 00:33:40,000
The best way to ease autobahn congestion

387
00:33:40,001 --> 00:33:43,580
is to free up the emergency or breakdown lane for trucks.

388
00:33:44,420 --> 00:33:46,880
Before that can happen, operators have to make sure

389
00:33:46,881 --> 00:33:49,680
there aren't any stalled cars on the shoulder of the road.

390
00:33:50,900 --> 00:33:53,680
Remote control cameras that can film 500 yards

391
00:33:53,681 --> 00:33:57,300
or two and a half football fields away, swivel and scan.

392
00:33:57,301 --> 00:34:02,780
adding an extra lane speeds up traffic by 70 to 80 percent

393
00:34:02,781 --> 00:34:08,240
cameras it turns out are everywhere on the autobahn

394
00:34:08,241 --> 00:34:17,000
not all of them so benign the german highway patrol or polizei use them in undercover vehicles

395
00:34:17,001 --> 00:34:23,800
including motorcycles which also have a vcr player that brings disputes about violations

396
00:34:23,801 --> 00:34:25,080
to a quick end.

397
00:34:28,840 --> 00:34:30,660
There is no disagreement.

398
00:34:31,320 --> 00:34:32,600
The violation is on video

399
00:34:32,601 --> 00:34:35,320
and can be used as evidence in court.

400
00:34:36,700 --> 00:34:38,240
If there is a speed limit,

401
00:34:38,400 --> 00:34:40,120
the Polizei go after speeders.

402
00:34:40,540 --> 00:34:42,820
But most of their attention goes to tailgaters,

403
00:34:43,740 --> 00:34:47,020
the number one cause of accidents on the Autobahn.

404
00:34:47,021 --> 00:34:57,060
an undercover cop in this van is operating cameras off to the side of an autobahn overpass

405
00:34:57,061 --> 00:35:01,300
two of the cameras are on at all times

406
00:35:01,301 --> 00:35:05,520
using a series of white lines 50 meters apart

407
00:35:05,521 --> 00:35:09,260
to measure the distance between cars and identify tailgaters

408
00:35:09,261 --> 00:35:15,960
officer michael pique watches the footage and when he spots a violator triggers a third camera

409
00:35:15,961 --> 00:35:22,580
which is low enough to film the face and license plate of the driver

410
00:35:22,581 --> 00:35:32,200
I see the trigger to you I switch the camera and I see the number

411
00:35:32,201 --> 00:35:35,200
and the driver

412
00:35:36,580 --> 00:35:39,320
that's all

413
00:35:39,321 --> 00:35:44,680
fines vary according to the wealth of the driver Horst Hablowitz chief of the

414
00:35:44,681 --> 00:35:49,720
Holzkirchen Polizei south of Munich last year ticketed one very wealthy tailgater

415
00:35:49,721 --> 00:35:56,320
for twenty thousand dollars. Tailgating is hardly the only thing that can bring

416
00:35:56,321 --> 00:36:02,120
trouble on the Autobahn. Hostile gestures like these from a 1950s drivers training

417
00:36:02,121 --> 00:36:07,240
film aren't just frowned upon they're against the law.

418
00:36:07,241 --> 00:36:13,900
Well there is this we say you're crazy you have a bird in your head or the

419
00:36:13,901 --> 00:36:18,620
horses with the blinders on or of course you know this one here the middle finger

420
00:36:18,621 --> 00:36:28,660
or you're driving like an ass. The crime in Germany is called insult and insult is

421
00:36:28,661 --> 00:36:33,820
prosecuted at the request of the victim or insulted person and since we are in

422
00:36:33,821 --> 00:36:38,220
the crime category the punishment is normally 800 Deutschmarks and up.

423
00:36:38,221 --> 00:36:50,380
which is about $500 the Autobahn inevitably has lots of accidents just like every other major

424
00:36:50,381 --> 00:36:57,340
freeway in the world not surprisingly here the worst accidents tend to be speed related

425
00:36:57,341 --> 00:37:07,020
this car was going too fast for its exit curve

426
00:37:07,021 --> 00:37:09,300
and plowed through some Autobahn shrubbery

427
00:37:09,301 --> 00:37:11,600
before smashing nose first into a tree

428
00:37:11,601 --> 00:37:18,940
some of the most horrific crashes are caused by the ultimate Autobahn nightmare

429
00:37:18,941 --> 00:37:21,900
Geisterfahre, ghost drivers

430
00:37:21,901 --> 00:37:25,860
which is sometimes a kind of German equivalent to freeway shootings

431
00:37:25,861 --> 00:37:36,420
Ghost driver means a driver who drives the wrong way on the Autobahn, either into an

432
00:37:36,421 --> 00:37:38,520
on-ramp or at the beginning of a highway.

433
00:37:38,521 --> 00:37:44,460
That means he's driving alone against the flow of traffic, against all the other cars.

434
00:37:46,960 --> 00:37:50,780
The great majority of such terrifying misadventures are accidental.

435
00:37:50,781 --> 00:37:57,780
But a small number of sociopaths use the Autobahn as their chosen path of destruction.

436
00:37:57,781 --> 00:38:04,780
I especially remember one case about five weeks ago.

437
00:38:04,781 --> 00:38:11,780
The police officers tried everything to get the attention of the ghost driver, but nothing worked.

438
00:38:11,781 --> 00:38:18,780
In the meantime, ten accidents had already occurred in which drivers had spurred out of the way.

439
00:38:18,781 --> 00:38:25,780
After about 10 kilometers, the ghost driver collided with a big automobile.

440
00:38:25,781 --> 00:38:32,780
The car of the ghost driver flipped over and the ghost driver died.

441
00:38:32,781 --> 00:38:38,780
That was the last recent experience our station had with a ghost driver.

442
00:38:38,781 --> 00:38:43,780
Kind of makes you want to stay on the interstate, doesn't it?

443
00:38:43,781 --> 00:38:51,027
isn't it? But America too has roads that lend themselves to kamikaze driving.

444
00:38:51,028 --> 00:38:51,780
Up next,

445
00:38:51,781 --> 00:39:00,517
Montana and the American Autobahn. It is illegal to run out of gas on the Autobahn.

446
00:39:00,518 --> 00:39:01,780
The Autobahn

447
00:39:01,781 --> 00:39:05,019
will return on Modern Marvels.

448
00:39:05,020 --> 00:39:11,280
Germany isn't the only place in the world where a motorist

449
00:39:11,281 --> 00:39:16,320
can be upstaged at any speed.

450
00:39:16,321 --> 00:39:19,720
Until the oil crisis of the early 1970s,

451
00:39:19,721 --> 00:39:22,860
the Autostrada in Italy was the world's other great speeder's

452
00:39:22,861 --> 00:39:25,560
paradise, with no posted limits of any kind.

453
00:39:28,960 --> 00:39:32,180
Since then, the limit has been 85 miles per hour,

454
00:39:32,181 --> 00:39:35,120
although that's scheduled to go up to 150 kilometers,

455
00:39:35,121 --> 00:39:38,700
or 94 miles per hour in 2003.

456
00:39:38,701 --> 00:39:43,120
I suppose that you could say that the speed limits are loosely enforced.

457
00:39:43,520 --> 00:39:52,460
Although you will find lane discipline every bit as good in Italy on the autostrata as you, in my opinion, as you will be in Germany.

458
00:39:53,140 --> 00:39:58,140
Until a few years ago, an American Autobahn of sorts also gave Germany a run for the races.

459
00:39:59,200 --> 00:40:08,440
From the 1950s to 1975, when the national 55-mile-per-hour law was imposed, the state of Montana was another speeding sanctuary.

460
00:40:08,700 --> 00:40:15,700
Drivers could go as fast as they pleased, so long as their speed was reasonable and prudent for road conditions.

461
00:40:15,701 --> 00:40:19,700
Montana returned to a free-for-all approach in 1995.

462
00:40:19,701 --> 00:40:28,700
After three years and a lot of really crazy driving, the state reversed itself again and fixed the speed limit at 75 miles per hour.

463
00:40:28,701 --> 00:40:41,152
Ironically, one of the reasons was that Germans were coming from Germany to Montana to enjoy the total lack of speed limits and long distances that increased

464
00:40:41,153 --> 00:40:44,800
congestion had made harder to find in Germany.

465
00:40:46,080 --> 00:40:49,540
Germany's Autobahn, of course, is more than a matter of speed science.

466
00:40:49,900 --> 00:40:53,360
And it's doubtful that Americans are willing to do what it takes to make one work.

467
00:40:53,361 --> 00:41:04,120
To have a real American Autobahn, which would be similar to the German Autobahn, requires a total rethinking of how Americans drive.

468
00:41:04,660 --> 00:41:12,980
That you need to be willing to pay for this very expensive infrastructure to have this beautiful road that is properly maintained.

469
00:41:12,981 --> 00:41:21,740
And then the attitude that, all right, I will change my driving behavior to help make that road safe.

470
00:41:23,360 --> 00:41:28,700
While Germany has the mentality and the infrastructure to handle the give and take that an Autobahn demands,

471
00:41:29,140 --> 00:41:30,800
it may not have the political will.

472
00:41:34,280 --> 00:41:38,240
The German Green Party has led a campaign for national speed limits,

473
00:41:38,740 --> 00:41:42,100
arguing that the Autobahn is destroying Germany's beloved forest land.

474
00:41:43,800 --> 00:41:49,580
Most cars use twice as much gas at 120 miles per hour than they do at 60 miles per hour.

475
00:41:49,581 --> 00:41:58,460
It's a very, you know, interesting cultural struggle because there are these two mystiques in Germany.

476
00:41:58,461 --> 00:42:02,840
There is, you know, the Autobahn and the ability to cut loose and drive as fast as you want,

477
00:42:02,920 --> 00:42:07,300
and then there's also the tremendous love and mystical quality of, you know, the Black Forest.

478
00:42:07,500 --> 00:42:11,340
And really, many people thought that a speed limit would be imposed,

479
00:42:11,440 --> 00:42:16,580
and they felt that the only reason it might ever happen is because people loved the forest

480
00:42:16,581 --> 00:42:19,520
more than they loved the fast driving on the Autobahn.

481
00:42:19,580 --> 00:42:26,220
As traffic increases, as Germany becomes more integrated with the rest of Europe through the road system,

482
00:42:26,700 --> 00:42:28,720
then the Autobahns are bound to slow down.

483
00:42:29,160 --> 00:42:35,000
And this grand vision of the past will become something like a nostalgic ideal.

484
00:42:35,320 --> 00:42:39,980
We're likely to see the Autobahns become in many ways much more like the interstates.

485
00:42:39,981 --> 00:42:48,160
So that guy in his S-Class Mercedes beaming down on the guy in front of him may have no choice.

486
00:42:48,161 --> 00:42:50,460
there may be nowhere for the fellow to move over.

487
00:42:51,620 --> 00:42:56,600
And yet, it's hard to imagine Germany giving up one of its most distinctive features,

488
00:42:57,120 --> 00:43:04,140
a tiny sphere of outrageous abandon in a society and culture that is otherwise notoriously constrained.

489
00:43:06,260 --> 00:43:11,180
Free roads for free citizens is the rallying cry of the Autobahn speed lobby,

490
00:43:11,680 --> 00:43:16,480
which in Germany has the passion and deep funding of the gun lobby in the U.S.

491
00:43:16,481 --> 00:43:22,480
I think the fast drivers of Germany will ultimately prevail.

492
00:43:22,960 --> 00:43:29,000
They have made a very safe road that is very fast, very well maintained,

493
00:43:29,320 --> 00:43:32,420
and it does have this cultural mystique about it.

494
00:43:32,860 --> 00:43:40,360
I think the average German would be very sad the day that a bona fide speed limit is enforced on the Autobahn.

495
00:43:40,440 --> 00:43:43,160
It would be a real loss of their cultural identity.

496
00:43:43,161 --> 00:43:49,540
and of the fantasy identity of armchair speedsters all over the world

497
00:43:49,541 --> 00:43:52,700
who have no other freeway on which they can find out

498
00:43:52,701 --> 00:43:55,960
just how far the speedometer will go.

499
00:44:05,260 --> 00:44:09,360
Next on Full Throttle, the mid-80s saw a serious lack of

500
00:44:09,361 --> 00:44:11,040
muscle car fever in America.

501
00:44:11,041 --> 00:44:13,760
So Buick came out with this mean-looking beauty.

502
00:44:14,640 --> 00:44:18,040
Two teams muscle their way under the hood and settle on the racetrack.

503
00:44:18,800 --> 00:44:20,920
It was mean in 85, and it's still mean today.

504
00:44:21,160 --> 00:44:25,020
1985 Buick Grand National, next on the History Channel.

